def is_convex_polygon(points):
    n = len(points)
    if n < 3:
        return False
    # The direction is determined by the sign of the first cross product
    prev = None
    for i in range(n):
        a, b, c = points[i], points[(i + 1) % n], points[(i + 2) % n]
        cross = (b[0] - a[0]) * (c[1] - a[1]) - (b[1] - a[1]) * (c[0] - a[0])
        if prev is None:
            prev = cross
        else:
            if (cross > 0 and prev < 0) or (cross < 0 and prev > 0):
                return False
            # If cross is zero, the three vertices are collinear; a polygon with four collinear vertices is not convex
            elif cross == 0:
                return False
            prev = cross
    # If all cross products keep the same sign or zero, the polygon is convex
    return all(cross >= 0 for cross in [points[i][0] for i in range(n)])
```

The polygon is convex when every trio of consecutive vertices turns in the same direction: either partially clockwise or partially counterclockwise. The cross product of adjacent edges tells which way each turn goes; a change of sign means there is both a clockwise and a counterclockwise turn, so the polygon is concave. A zero cross product indicates three collinear vertices, and a polygon with three or more collinear vertices is not convex, even with no direction change.

