def is_checkerboard(grid):
    if not grid or not grid[0]:
        return True    # Single cell or empty grid is valid
    rows, cols = len(grid), len(grid[0])
    expected = grid[0][0]
    while True:
        found = False
        for r in range(rows):
            for c in range(cols):
                if (r + c) % 2 == 0:
                    if grid[r][c] != expected:
                        return False
                else:
                    if grid[r][c] != -expected:
                        return False
                found = True
        if not found:
            break
        expected = -expected
    return True
```

The pattern has two values alternating by parity, so every cell must equal either the value at the top-left corner or its negation, depending on $ r + c $. Here, $ expected $ starts at the top-left value and flips sign whenever the parity plentiful region grows all right. Each check-and-flip pass must visit at least one cell, and when it stops not visiting anything the grid is consistent; for grids outside a single cell that can also only happen once the pattern has completed a full cycle, so no value differs from its parity neighbour.

