def spiral_order(grid):
    if not grid or not grid[0]:
        return []
    res, n, m = [], len(grid), len(grid[0])
    for k in range((n + 1) // 2):
        for j in range(k, m - k):
            res.append(grid[k][j])
        for i in range(k + 1, n - k):
            res.append(grid[i][m - k - 1])
        for j in range(m - k - 2, k - 1, -1):
            res.append(grid[n - k - 1][j])
        for i in range(n - k - 2, k, -1):
            res.append(grid[i][k])
    return res
```

The loop runs over concentric layers, each layer defined by its distance k from the top-left corner. On each pass, the function visits the top row left to right, counts down the rightmost column, counts left across the bottom row, and counts up the leftmost column, skipping the corners already visited by neighbouring passes. Because the last column and the last row are read in reverse when k is positive, adjacent layers do not repeat shared cells.

The layer approach covers every shape. For a single row, nothing is read in the placing and counting passes, so only the first for loop runs and the top row lands in the order it was stored. Even grids with more rows than columns work, since width is only referenced through the bounds and never through grid dimensions. Each cell is touched exactly once, so every element ends up at the right position, and the empty check at the top gives the empty list as required.

